From c389c747ca777de7b7e10e2507ebed7b96b36a05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Garcia <44309076+ElGuslml@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 21:46:11 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 17/24] gpu: skip ownership transfers into render targets a
 draw fully overwrites

When a draw binds a render target whose EDRAM range is owned by another
host render target, the render target cache copies the previous owner's
contents into it first (an ownership transfer: a full-screen draw per
range, with sample reconstruction when the MSAA modes differ). That copy
is wasted when the draw replaces every sample of the range without
reading it.

Games clear EDRAM this way: Pirates of the Caribbean: At World's End
draws one huge rectangle through a 4x MSAA surface at half the pitch
(the same tile layout as its 2x MSAA scene surface at the full pitch),
19 times per frame. Each clear copied the whole 2x MSAA color and depth
buffers into the 4x MSAA alias and back; at a 2x2 resolution scale those
transfers were about a third of the GPU time (20.4 ms -> 15.8 ms per
frame on an RTX 4070 Ti, the copies back remain).

New cvar render_target_skip_transfers_on_overwrite (default off). When
set, RenderTargetCache::Update checks whether the draw is a full
overwrite of each bound render target and, if so, changes the ownership
of the range with a cutout of the whole range, as resolve clears do, so
no transfer into it is recorded:

- Output state (GetFullyOverwrittenRenderTargets): the pixel shader does
  not discard, coverage does not depend on alpha; depth: test always
  passing with writes enabled, stencil enabled, always passing, replaced
  or zeroed with a full write mask (both faces when back-face stencil is
  enabled); color: all four components written with ONE/ZERO/ADD
  blending.
- Geometry (DrawExtentEstimator::IsFullCoverageDraw): one triangle or
  rectangle (3 indices) whose vertices, run through the vertex shader on
  the CPU with the interpreter like EstimateVertexMaxY, form a convex
  polygon containing the four corners of the EDRAM tile-aligned range of
  the render target in screen space (viewport, window offset and pixel
  center applied); the scissor contains that rectangle; no culling,
  wireframe, tessellation, user clip planes or vertex kill; with clipping
  enabled, the viewport must contain the rectangle and every vertex must
  be in the depth range. Anything that cannot be proven returns false.

Only the transfer into the render target is skipped; the later transfer
of the cleared contents back to the scene's render target is still done.
The pixel shader is now passed to RenderTargetCache::Update (both
backends) for the discard check.

